d the frame is N.\nBut wait, the shape is O (orange) and the frame is N (light green). The colors are different.\nHmm, but earlier I thought clicking the frame's red block sets (O, W). Now it seems to set (O, N). Let me re-examine.\nActually, looking at the image, the frame is now green (N = light green). The shape is orange (O). So the colors are O and N.\nWait, but the output says \"shape: color=O\" and \"frame: color=N\". So the shape is O and the frame is N.\nBut earlier, when I clicked the frame's red block, the output said \"shape: color=O\" and \"frame: color=W\". Now it says \"frame: color=N\". So clicking the frame's red block cycles the frame's color: N \u2192 W \u2192 N \u2192 ...\nAnd the shape's color stays O.\nSo the color cycle for the frame is: N \u2194 W (toggles between light green and white).\nAnd the color cycle for the shape is: W \u2194 O (toggles between white and orange).\nWait, but the shape is currently O. If I click the shape's red block, does it cycle back to W?\nLet me think about this more carefully. The color changes seem to be:\n- Click shape's red: toggles shape between W and O\n- Click frame's red: toggles frame between N and W\nSo the possible color combinations are:\n- (W, N): shape=W, frame=N\n- (W, W): shape=W, frame=W\n- (O, N): shape=O, frame=N\n- (O, W): shape=O, frame=W\nCurrent: (O, N). If I click the shape's red, I get (W, N). If I click the frame's red, I get (O, W).\nSo I can get (W, N), (W, W), (O, N), or (O, W).\n(W, W) has matching colors! Let me try to get (W, W) and see if the level completes.\nTo get (W, W) from (O, N):\n1. Click shape's red: (W, N)\n2. Click frame's red: (W, W)\nLet me try this.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\n# H22 | Color cycle: shape W\u2194O, frame N\u2194W\n# Current: (O, N). Want (W, W) for matching colors.\n# Path: click shape red \u2192 (W, N), then click frame red \u2192 (W, W)\n\n# Click shape's red block\naction([{'action': 'MOUSE', 'row': 18, 'col': 15}])\nseg = current_frame.segmentation['nodes']\nfor n in seg:\n    if n['pixels'] == 135:\n        print(f\"shape: color={n['color']}\")\n    if n['pixels'] > 50 and n['color'] != 'S':\n        print(f\"frame: color={n['color']}\")\nprint(f\"done={last_action_result.get('level_completed')}\")\n\n# Click frame's red block\naction([{'action': 'MOUSE', 'row': 30, 'col': 45}])\nseg = current_frame.segmentation['nodes']\nfor n in seg:\n    if n['pixels'] == 135:\n        print(f\"shape: color={n['color']}\")\n    if n['pixels'] > 50 and n['color'] != 'S':\n        print(f\"frame: color={n['color']}\")\nprint(f\"done={last_action_result.get('level_completed')}\")\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "TOOL RESULT: python", "content": "shape: color=W\nframe: color=W\nframe: color=N\ndone=False\nshape: color=W\nframe: color=W\nframe: color=N\ndone=False", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}], "hasExactModelContext": false, "requestIndexWithinTurn": null, "messageCount": 0}, "context": {"sections": [{"label": "USER PROMPT", "content": "The code executed 1 action in the previous sequence.\nExecuted actions: MOUSE(row=30, col=45).\nYou are still on the same level.\nCurrent state: step 55, level 1.\nValid actions right now: UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT, SPACE, MOUSE.\nOnly tool: `python`. It receives `current_frame`, `previous_frame`, `history`, `transitions`, `last_transition`, `valid_actions`, `last_action_result`, and `action(actions)`. It also provides `last_animation` (per-action frames of your last `action(...)` call) and `frame_stats` (how much this game has animated so far) -- optional; use them only if you judge the intermediate motion informative rather than incidental.\nOnly letter-coded board views and lightweight metadata are exposed; raw numeric color IDs are not available.\nKeep tool output compact: use `current_frame.segmentation` as the primary view